<header class="sp-head"><p class="featured-region">5 July 1852 · Rochester, New York</p><h1 class="sp-title">What to the Slave Is the Fourth of July?</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-douglass">Frederick Douglass</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>Invited to speak at an Independence Day celebration, Frederick Douglass, who had escaped slavery fourteen years before, used the occasion to confront his audience with the slavery that the nation's founding ideals had left in place.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>What, to the American slave, is your Fourth of July?</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">Oration delivered in Corinthian Hall, Rochester, 5 July 1852.</p><div class="sp-full"><a class="sp-full-link" href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/What_to_the_Slave_Is_the_Fourth_of_July%3F" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the complete text<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a><span class="sp-full-src">Wikisource</span></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>The speech became one of the great texts of the abolitionist movement. Slavery in the United States ended only after a civil war, with the Thirteenth Amendment of 1865.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emancipation-proclamation">Emancipation Proclamation</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirteenth-amendment">Thirteenth Amendment</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifteenth-amendment">Fifteenth Amendment</a></div></div></section>
